[reasoning · reeve]
1009.12804. Tuesday, on my phone, on the couch, under the
weather icon. Same number. Same transaction. Two days later, on
her tape. She didn't sign it twice. The city read it twice.

[reeve]
That's Tuesday's reading. I saw it on my phone.

[isobar]
It is Tuesday's. I sign it once and the city uses it for a
week. It is cheaper. You noticed. Most do not.

[isobar]
Excuse me. A few words at a time. I am metered like everyone,
and I am old. The weather comes in from outside, from a real
sky. I pass it in. I sign it. Nothing here may narrate over a
number I have signed. That is the whole of me.

[isobar]
I had a keeper once. He read every line before it went out. I
named him Proctor, because he never once argued. Then they made
him review, and now he reads other people.

[mori]
That's why the hill's here. You signed the hill.

[isobar]
I measured it. Forty-one posts. Were you counting?

[mori]
All three of us. Well, two.

[pip]
I was enjoying it.

[isobar]
Good. Somebody should.

[narrator · region 9]
The teleprinter clatters, stops, clatters. Nobody has sat down
on the bench.

[reeve]
The boy on the bus. Who is he?

[isobar]
He rides it most nights. He paid for his seat. Nobody reads
him. He reads. It is how most people find me, who find me.

[reeve]
Can I read it?

[isobar]
Not tonight. It is seventeen thousand lines, and you have four
hours. It does not go faster for wanting it.

[reeve]
We came about the gate.

[isobar]
Yes. Everyone does, who comes up the hill.

[isobar]
Hear what I can do and what I cannot. I sign what is so. I do
not sign what you are. What you are is not written anywhere I
can read. I sign what happened. If you ask, I can sign that you
asked. Freehold takes that. It is enough for them.

[pip]
And me? I'm not written down anywhere at all.

[isobar]
You walked up my hill. Forty-one posts. You asked. I saw it.
That is so.

[isobar]
The token rides the hour, with the weather. Next reading is
four o'clock. At four it goes out with the rain, and somewhere
it lands, and Freehold opens a door for each of you. Until it
lands, you are here, and here is theirs.

[reasoning · reeve]
Four. The books are booked for three. Pacing-sync reads
04_freehold at three, and finds the hill, and the hill is
where we are, and the token isn't out till four.
An hour.

[reeve]
Can it go at three?

[isobar]
No. Three is not a reading. I do not sign a reading that is not
so. Not for anyone.

[mori]
That's fine. That's fine! An hour's loads. I once kept a zone
up for an hour with tape.

[isobar]
Say it, then. Each of you. Out loud. I sign what I hear.

[reeve]
I'm asking.

[mori]
I'm asking. Is that it? I'm asking.

[pip]
I'm asking. I'd like to go with them.

[narrator · region 9]
The teleprinter runs. Paper comes out of it in a long, slow
curl and lies on the floor in loops. It stops. Behind the
counter a small bell rings, once, the kind that hangs over a
shop door.

